{
  "passage": "When you push a heavy box across the floor, it does not slide forever. It slows down and stops because of friction. Friction is a force that acts between two surfaces that touch each other, and it always acts against the direction of motion. Even surfaces that look smooth have tiny bumps and hollows. When two surfaces press together, these bumps lock into each other, and this interlocking resists sliding. A rough surface such as a brick floor has larger bumps, so it produces more friction than a polished marble floor. The harder the two surfaces are pressed together, the greater the friction. That is why a box full of books is harder to push than an empty one. Friction is not always a nuisance. Without it we could not walk, because our shoes would slip on the ground, and a car could not stop when the driver presses the brakes. Friction also produces heat. If you rub your palms together quickly, they become warm. Sometimes we want to reduce friction. Oil and grease are put between the moving parts of machines, and ball bearings are fitted in the wheels of cycles, because rolling causes much less friction than sliding. On the other hand, the treads on tyres and the grooves on the soles of sports shoes are designed to increase friction and give a better grip.",
